(FMT-57 — LANGUAGE.md:3418 (new in 0.4.14, #108). "A text variable
 reassigned from a format string releases the string it no longer
 holds." The fix itself (freeing the old string on `Set <text> to
 "<format>"`, instead of leaking one buffer per evaluation) is a memory
 claim not observable from inside Vox — no leak-detection channel. This
 probe hand-verifies the one thing that IS observable: the accumulate
 idiom the CHANGELOG names by name (`Set acc to "{acc}x"` in a loop)
 still produces the correct value after repeated reassignment, i.e. the
 fix did not corrupt the assignment path while changing its allocator
 behavior underneath.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/usr/share/vox/coreasm /usr/bin/vox FMT-57.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   startxxxxx
)
a text called acc is "start".
a number called n is 0.
While n is less than 5,
    Set acc to "{acc}x",
    increment n.
Print acc.
